+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Workers can edit copies of the same vegetation visit while tablets are disconnected for up to 48 hours. A visit contains scientifically related values: a species identification can affect the meaning of its cover estimate, notes can qualify structured observations, and observer corrections can change how coordinators interpret the record. The server must preserve submitted work, establish an inspectable exported value, and avoid treating network arrival order as scientific authority.
+
+The concurrency policy determines more than storage behavior. Tablet-sync must carry whatever context the policy needs while offline, and review-export must expose its unresolved states and keep them out of the partner CSV. The policy therefore needs predictable downstream obligations that a small team can operate without maintaining hidden scientific inference in software.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Preserve every accepted submission as a gate.** An option is eliminated if reconciliation can overwrite or erase a worker's submitted values and provenance.
+- **Avoid unreviewed scientific inference as a major criterion.** This follows the project tenets to keep submitted-to-exported values inspectable and to avoid silent loss. Reconciliation should not imply that independently edited fields are scientifically independent unless a person verifies that judgment.
+- **Coordinator workload as a major criterion.** Two coordinators must clear review work before the weekly export; unnecessary contention has a recurring operational cost.
+- **Offline client burden as a moderate criterion.** Twelve shared tablets need durable retry state through long disconnections, but the record volume permits storing complete snapshots.
+- **Explainability as a major criterion.** A coordinator must be able to reconstruct which complete observations were considered and how the exported revision resulted.
+- **Rule-maintenance burden as a moderate criterion.** The team has no dedicated systems administrator, and vegetation record structure will evolve.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+**Description.** Each edit submits a complete visit and names its base revision. An uncontested edit advances the visit. Divergent branch tips remain intact and enter one review case against their neutral base: the closest shared ancestor that can remain usable without preferring either branch. Competing initial creations have no neutral base. A coordinator selects the neutral base or a contender, or authors a replacement after comparing the reviewed revisions. No server process merges individual fields.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **passes preserve every accepted submission:** every submitted snapshot remains an immutable revision in history.
+- **strong on avoiding unreviewed scientific inference:** only a coordinator can combine observations from competing revisions.
+- **weak on coordinator workload:** edits to unrelated fields still create review work, and every open case blocks export for that visit.
+- **strong on offline client burden:** tablets retain a base revision ID and complete payload but do not compute structured patches or field provenance.
+- **strong on explainability:** review and history operate on the same complete snapshots workers submitted.
+- **strong on rule-maintenance burden:** the store has conflict-state rules but no field-specific merge policy to update as the schema changes.
+
+**Summary.** This option buys conservative scientific handling and simple provenance with a larger review queue.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+**Description.** Each edit can submit the same base revision ID and complete edited payload used by whole-visit revisions; the server retrieves the stored base snapshot and compares it with the current and incoming values. Structured client-side changes are an optional optimization, not a prerequisite. Changes to fields classified as independent combine automatically; overlapping changes enter field-level review. Lists such as observers and keyed species entries require dedicated identity, insertion, deletion, and overlap rules. Provenance attaches to each resulting field or collection entry.
+
+This remains a credible future option because most edits may prove mechanically independent in practice. If review volume threatens the Friday export, measured edit patterns could justify automating well-understood cases and materially reducing coordinator work.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **passes preserve every accepted submission:** original payloads can remain immutable even when the store derives a merged representation.
+- **weak on avoiding unreviewed scientific inference:** non-overlapping storage fields can still be scientifically related, so a syntactically valid merge can create a combination no worker intended.
+- **strong on coordinator workload:** genuinely disjoint corrections become exportable without entering the queue.
+- **strong on offline client burden:** server-side comparison can use the same tablet envelope as whole-visit revisions; client-side patches are optional and carry no weight in selecting this option.
+- **partial on explainability:** field-level provenance can explain mechanical origins, but the resulting visit may never have existed as one worker's complete observation.
+- **weak on rule-maintenance burden:** scalar fields, observer lists, species entries, and future schema additions each need maintained merge and overlap rules.
+
+**Summary.** This option buys a smaller queue and faster export readiness with field-level provenance machinery and scientific assumptions in server code.
+
+## Selection
+
+Use whole-visit revisions. Avoiding unreviewed scientific inference and retaining complete submitted observations outweigh the recurring cost of reviewing edits that might have merged safely. This turns most directly on the tenets to keep the submitted-to-exported path inspectable and not silently discard edits. The team accepts a larger queue rather than maintaining rules that can combine scientifically related changes without recognizing their relationship.
+
+Tablet-sync must persist a complete payload, its base revision ID, and stable idempotency information until the server result is known. It must handle three outcomes without inventing merge behavior: the edit becomes current, the edit enters review against the closest shared ancestor, or the visit has no current state because initial creations compete. Offline retry and user messaging must preserve those distinctions.
+
+The record store preserves reviewed and contributing revision provenance. Review-export must retrieve and display that provenance, compare complete revisions and their common base, allow a coordinator to select the neutral base or a contender or author a complete replacement, refresh and retry safely when the exact competitor set changes, and exclude every open case from CSV output. It cannot export the neutral base merely because that snapshot remains usable during review.
+
+Field-aware merging remains available as a later replacement if observed queue volume makes whole-visit review operationally unsustainable. Adopting it would require a new decision with measured conflict patterns, explicit scientific ownership of merge rules, field-level provenance, and migration semantics for existing revision history. The existing tablet envelope could remain intact if merging stays server-side; structured client changes would require a separate justification.
