 This node owns visit persistence and the meaning of a current visit when independently edited copies arrive. A visit is identified by a client-generated UUID and refers to a catalog plot ID. Updates can arrive days after their starting state was fetched. The server must not treat arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
 
-Tablet-sync and review-export are not yet designed. Their contracts depend on the concurrency mechanism selected here. Neither the HLD nor the catalog contract selects that mechanism.
+The store uses whole-visit revisions. Every accepted edit remains an immutable complete snapshot; the store advances an uncontested revision automatically and sends competing revisions to coordinator review. It does not merge fields or infer relationships among scientific observations.
 
 ## Architecture
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, revision-history retrieval, and coordinator resolution operations. A visit has zero or one current revision and zero or one open review case. A visit normally lacks a current revision only when competing initial creations have no shared base.
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+Revisions, submissions, review cases, and resolutions are separate records. A worker revision stores a complete visit payload, its server-generated revision ID, visit ID, base revision ID, submitting actor, receipt time, and submission ID. A coordinator-authored revision stores the same visit and payload data, identifies its source as a resolution instead of a submission, uses the case's neutral base as its base revision, and records the reviewed revisions from which its contributors were selected. Revisions are immutable, including after review.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+The current revision is the store's usable neutral snapshot, not a claim that all review is complete. A review case stores its neutral base revision, which is the closest shared ancestor of the revisions that caused contention, plus every competing revision accepted before resolution. During review, the neutral base is current and competing revisions are not. When competing initial creations have no shared ancestor, the case has no neutral base and the visit has no current revision. Every open case makes its visit ineligible for export.
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+## Submission Contract
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Each submission envelope contains:
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+- an idempotency key scoped to the authenticated actor;
+- the client-generated visit ID;
+- the base revision ID from which the complete payload was edited, or `null` when creating a visit;
+- the complete visit payload, including plot ID, observation date, observers, species cover estimates, and notes.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+The store authenticates the actor and validates the complete payload and plot reference before accepting it. A non-null base revision must exist and belong to the submitted visit. A null base denotes an independently created visit. It creates the first root when the visit is new; when the visit already has revision history, it is accepted as a competing root regardless of whether earlier root contention has already been resolved.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Acceptance creates one immutable revision and returns its revision ID, whether it became current, and any open review-case ID. The transition depends on the visit state:
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+| Visit state at acceptance | Result |
+|---|---|
+| No current revision, first accepted creation | The new revision becomes current. |
+| No open review case and base equals current | The new revision becomes current. |
+| No open review case and base differs from current | A case opens for the incumbent current revision and the new revision. Their closest shared ancestor becomes current when one exists; otherwise the visit has no current revision. Each branch tip that differs from the neutral base becomes a competitor. |
+| Open review case | The new revision joins the case. The store recomputes the closest shared ancestor across the case's reviewed branches. That revision becomes the neutral current when one exists; otherwise the visit has no current revision. A former neutral base that is displaced becomes a competitor, and the new neutral base is excluded from the competitor set. |
+| Null-base creation for a visit with revision history and no open case | A root-contention case opens with no neutral base or current revision; the incumbent current revision and new root revision become competitors. |
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Submission acceptance is serialized with other submissions and resolutions for the same visit. The first operation to commit determines the state seen by the next operation; a submission committed before resolution joins that case and makes a resolution with an older expected set fail, while a submission committed after resolution is evaluated against the resolved state.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+A retry using an accepted idempotency key and the identical logical envelope returns the stored acceptance result without creating a revision or changing visit state. Logical equality compares structured values after object-key canonicalization while preserving array order. Concurrent uses of one key are serialized: an identical request replays the winner, while a different request is rejected. Rejected requests do not reserve a key. The acceptance result is stored atomically with the revision before a response is sent, so a lost response can be replayed.
+
+## Retrieval Contract
+
+Current-state retrieval returns one atomic snapshot containing the visit ID; the current revision ID and complete payload when one exists; and either no review case or the open case ID and the IDs, authors, and receipt times of all competing revisions. It does not substitute a competitor for the neutral base. An unknown visit returns a not-found result.
+
+Revision-history retrieval returns one atomic snapshot containing every accepted revision for the visit, including its complete payload and provenance metadata, ordered by receipt time and then revision ID. It also returns retained closed cases and their resolution records. Resolved competitors remain in history, and resolution does not rewrite their payloads or authorship. An unknown visit returns a not-found result.
+
+## Coordinator Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves the entire open case in one operation. The request carries an idempotency key, names the case ID, its neutral base revision when one exists, and its complete expected set of competing revision IDs, and selects one outcome:
+
+- select the current revision or one competing revision as the resulting current revision; or
+- provide a new complete visit payload derived by human review, with the contributing revision IDs identified.
+
+A replacement payload receives the same field and plot-reference validation as a worker submission and creates an immutable coordinator-authored revision. Every resolution records the authenticated coordinator, resolution time, neutral base when one exists, and every competitor as reviewed. Contributor IDs must be a nonempty subset of the reviewed revisions, where the reviewed set includes the neutral base when one exists. Selecting an existing revision records that revision as the sole contributor. A replacement revision uses the case's neutral base as its base, has no submission ID, and uses the resolution time as its receipt time.
+
+Resolution succeeds only for an authenticated coordinator, when the case is still open, its neutral base matches, its competing revision set exactly matches the request, and the selected and contributing revisions belong to the reviewed set. If another submission joined the case, the operation is rejected without changing the current revision or closing the case, so the coordinator cannot unknowingly omit a newly accepted competitor. A successful resolution atomically stores the resolution, sets the resulting current revision, and closes the case. The result is stored against the coordinator-scoped idempotency key before responding; a retry compares canonical structured values, treating competitor and contributor IDs as sets while preserving order within the replacement visit payload. An identical retry returns the stored result, while different content using that accepted key is rejected. Failed resolutions do not reserve an idempotency key. Later edits use the resulting revision ID as their base; stale edits can open a new case.
+
+Rejected operations return a stable machine-readable reason so callers can distinguish `UNAUTHENTICATED`, `FORBIDDEN`, `INVALID_PAYLOAD`, `INVALID_PLOT_REFERENCE`, `UNKNOWN_BASE_REVISION`, `BASE_VISIT_MISMATCH`, `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`, `VISIT_NOT_FOUND`, `REVIEW_CASE_NOT_OPEN`, `STALE_REVIEW_CASE`, and `INVALID_RESOLUTION_SELECTION`. Authentication failures can be retried after authentication, validation failures require corrected content, and stale-case failures require refreshed case state. No rejected operation reserves an idempotency key unless that key already belongs to an accepted operation.
+
+## Invariants and Failure Boundaries
+
+- A visit has at most one current revision and one open review case.
+- An open case has a neutral base, which is current when it exists, and a nonempty duplicate-free competitor set that excludes the neutral base. A newly accepted branch can move the neutral base to an earlier shared ancestor or remove it; the previous neutral then becomes a competitor.
+- Every accepted submission creates exactly one immutable revision, except an identical idempotent retry, which creates none.
+- Receipt order never selects among competing revisions.
+- No field-level merge occurs in submission or resolution processing; a human-authored replacement is a new complete revision with explicit contributors.
+- Submission, resolution, current-state retrieval, and revision-history retrieval observe a linearizable per-visit state.
+- Invalid authentication or authorization, payloads, plot references, base references, idempotency reuse, selected revisions, contributor sets, and stale resolution requests are rejected without changing visit state.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edits | [Whole-visit revision decision](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md) | Field-aware three-way merge of non-overlapping fields | Whole revisions preserve each worker's scientific observations as a unit and avoid merge rules that could combine related edits without recognizing their relationship. The larger review queue is acceptable for the two coordinators. |
+| State exposed during contention | Keep the closest shared ancestor current and mark the visit as having open review work | Keep the first-arriving branch current; make the visit have no current state | A shared ancestor is a usable neutral snapshot that does not give scientific authority to receipt order. Competing roots have no such snapshot and therefore have no current state. |
+| Resolution race handling | Require the exact competing-revision set | Resolve a snapshot while leaving later competitors for another case | Exact matching prevents a coordinator from closing review without seeing every submission already accepted into the case. |
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync must retain the base revision ID and complete payload for each local edit, use stable idempotency keys across retries, and explain whether an accepted revision became current or entered review. Review-export must compare complete revisions, submit race-safe resolutions, and exclude every visit with an open review case from CSV output. Those nodes define their own storage, interaction, and presentation details without changing the concurrency semantics owned here.
